The most common sources of subtle (i.e., difficult-to-spot) erroneous results in genomics workflows are those that allow an analysis to complete and produce plausible-looking output while silently corrupting the biological interpretation.  

- Mutually incompatible file formats almost invariably cause an immediate parser or tool failure; the error is therefore obvious and is not regarded as a “silent” failure mode.  
- In contrast, the three remaining problems routinely produce quiet corruptions:  
  – chromosome labels that differ only by the presence/absence of a “chr” prefix cause coordinate joins or interval overlaps to drop most (or all) records without raising an exception;  
  – mixing reference assemblies (hg19 versus GRCh38, for example) maps variant or peak calls to the wrong genomic loci while still yielding numerically valid tables;  
  – gene-identifier conversion (symbols \(\leftrightarrow\) Entrez \(\leftrightarrow\) Ensembl, etc.) frequently introduces many-to-many or outdated mappings that alter downstream enrichment or network results without any overt warning.  

Consequently only items 2, 3 and 4 satisfy the criterion of “difficult-to-spot erroneous results,” which corresponds to option (D).

\boxed{D}

